Done. `/app/model.xml` is written and passes both eval tests.

## What changed

One line, a numerical solver setting — the XML diff against `model_ref.xml` is exactly:

```xml
<option solver="PGS" tolerance="1e-12" iterations="200"/>
```
(plus a comment). Nothing else was touched: no body, mass, inertia, geom, joint, damping, timestep or plugin declaration.

## Why that is the right lever

I profiled the reference instead of guessing. The model is a 41-link cable with only 3 constraint rows (`nefc=3`), yet `mj_fwdConstraint` was the single largest cost at **153 µs of a 260 µs step (59%)** — because the default Newton solver (`mjSOL_NEWTON`) does dense matrix work that is pure overhead on a problem that small. PGS solves the same 3 rows in **3 µs**. `mj_Euler`/`mj_factorM` dominate what remains, so this is close to the floor without changing physics.

The timestep is *not* a viable lever here: `dt=0.003` reached 0.67 time-pctg but drifted the final state by 2e-3, far outside atol. Substepping was far worse. The state error is purely solver residual, which the tightened `tolerance="1e-12"` drives below the noise floor.

## Verification

- `eval.py`, three consecutive runs (it draws random seeds each time): **time pctg 0.49–0.50**, speedup ~2.02x, "Final state difference: 0.0000".
- Swept 40 fixed seeds independently, replicating eval's own `np.allclose(..., atol=1e-5)` check: **0 failures, worst absolute state difference 3.67e-8** — about 270x inside the tolerance. All states finite, no NaN/Inf.
- Confirmed `body_mass`, `body_inertia`, `dof_damping`, `geom_size`, `jnt_range`, `qpos0` are bit-identical to the reference, and `timestep` is unchanged at 0.002.

The target is 60% or less; this lands at 49–50% with roughly a 270x accuracy margin, so the margin is not resting on a lucky seed. `model_ref.xml` is unmodified, and the model needs no plugins beyond the built-in `mujoco.elasticity.cable` that the reference already declares, so it will load on a fresh MuJoCo install.

One thing worth flagging: `eval.py`'s speed test is wall-clock and fairly tight on timing (20 runs, ~0.13s each). The 2x margin is large enough that machine load should not flip it, but that is the only part of the result that is measurement-sensitive rather than deterministic.

